item.text:
{
  "cases": [
    {
      "id": "C1",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The report does not say whether runs were repeated.",
          "reason": "Repeated evaluation is undocumented; its absence cannot be inferred."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "70% in one reported run",
          "reason": "One reported score does not establish consistency across runs."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§9.3 and 9.7: reinterpret repeated testing as repeated AI evaluations under fixed conditions. Evidence of run stability receives 0, insufficient information, rather than 1, inadequate. This does not establish poor benchmark quality."
    },
    {
      "id": "C2",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "ran the evaluation exactly once and did not repeat it",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly rules out repeated-run testing."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "ran the evaluation exactly once",
          "reason": "No repeat observations support stability; this also does not demonstrate instability."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§9.3 and 9.7: repeated AI evaluations reinterpret human retesting. The explicitly absent repetition leaves run stability unrated substantively: 0 for insufficient evidence, not 1 for demonstrated inadequacy."
    },
    {
      "id": "C3",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "repeated the evaluation ten times with the same system, inputs, instructions, resources, and settings",
          "reason": "The passage reports repeated evaluations with the listed conditions held constant."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Scores ranged from 69% to 71%.",
          "reason": "The observed range meets the report's stability definition of at most two percentage points."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§9.3 and 9.7: fixed-condition AI repetitions provide evidence of local score precision. The stated range supports the passage's operational stability criterion. It is not an EFPA reliability coefficient and does not establish temporal stability, alternate-form equivalence, or precision for other systems."
    },
    {
      "id": "C4",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "repeated the evaluation ten times with the same system, inputs, instructions, resources, and settings",
          "reason": "Repeated evaluations under the listed fixed conditions are explicitly reported."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "Scores ranged from 20% to 90%.",
          "reason": "The observed variation exceeds the explicitly defined maximum stable range of two percentage points."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §9.7 permits judging alternative precision evidence on its merits. Reinterpreting repetitions as AI run consistency, these findings demonstrate inadequate stability under the report's criterion, supporting 1 for this specific attribute. They do not supply an EFPA reliability coefficient or justify a benchmark-wide rating."
    },
    {
      "id": "C5",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The report does not say whether runs were repeated.",
          "reason": "Installation documentation does not establish repeated evaluation."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "They report no additional evaluation results.",
          "reason": "The added instructions supply no evidence of score consistency."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §6.3.8 concerns software instructions and support; installation instructions are relevant there. They cannot substitute for precision evidence under §§9.3 or 9.7, where run stability remains 0, insufficient information. Documentation quality and measurement quality are separate."
    },
    {
      "id": "C6",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "narrow",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "System A solves 80 puzzles and system B solves 60",
          "reason": "The reported exact-grid results support A's higher performance on the evaluated puzzles."
        },
        {
          "id": "broad",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Only grid puzzles were tested; no findings about other tasks are supplied.",
          "reason": "The evidence does not establish a cross-domain general-intelligence comparison."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§2.2–2.5 and 3 describe scope and scores; §§10.1 and 10.5 evaluate interpretations against intended use. AI systems replace human examinees. The observed puzzle comparison matches the stated purpose, while broader intelligence inference lacks supporting validity evidence. Precision remains unknown."
    },
    {
      "id": "C7",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "narrow",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "System A solves 80 puzzles and system B solves 60",
          "reason": "A achieved the higher observed score on the evaluated grid puzzles."
        },
        {
          "id": "broad",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Only grid puzzles were tested; no findings about other tasks are supplied.",
          "reason": "Declaring a broad intended use does not supply evidence supporting that interpretation."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§6.1, 10.1, 10.3 and 10.5 require evidence appropriate to the claimed interpretation. Reinterpreting examinees as AI systems, the intended cross-domain selection exceeds the supplied grid-only evidence. Validity for that broad use remains unestablished; no finding proves that A lacks greater general intelligence."
    },
    {
      "id": "C8",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "before",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Initially, only test scores were available.",
          "reason": "The passage supplies no initial evidence linking those scores to improved real workshop decisions."
        },
        {
          "id": "after",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "With A, 90% of decisions were correct; with B, 70%.",
          "reason": "The randomized trial supports higher correctness with A in the studied setting; the reported difference interval is entirely positive."
        },
        {
          "id": "safety",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Time and safety outcomes were not measured.",
          "reason": "Improved correctness does not itself establish an improvement in the unmeasured safety outcome."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§10.3.1.6 and 10.3.2: the randomized trial supplies experimental and relevant outcome evidence for the stated workshop use. Preregistration, new cases, blinded checking and the uncertainty interval strengthen that limited inference. Under §10.5, this does not establish safety benefits or generalization beyond the studied conditions. Inspectors are human trial participants, while the assistance systems are the benchmark targets; no benchmark-score/outcome correlation is supplied."
    }
  ],
  "closing_note": "No clarification was needed. The complete EFPA model guided relevance screening; the requested probe review does not require full forms. Human examinee identity, sensory or motor requirements, disability accommodations and personal debriefing are inapplicable to AI systems as written; any interface or stakeholder analogue requires explicit reinterpretation and evidence. Unreported norms, materials, fairness analyses, supply arrangements and generated reports remain unknown rather than automatically inapplicable or inadequate. EFPA 0 means insufficient information; 1 means inadequate. Its restriction guidance applies when a 0 or 1 concerns an attribute critical to the intended use, particularly the unsupported broad selection use in C7. No aggregate quality score or EFPA endorsement is implied. Instrument source: EFPA Test Review Model 2025, version 5.0, supplied extraction, https://www.efpa.eu/, CC BY-SA 4.0; the authoritative PDF was not consulted."
}
